  <constraints>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 round 2, general">Step 0 merges origin/master into the branch (including #2150 once it lands) before any edit. POMLs and notes cite symbols, not line numbers. Every edit to a uac-r2 file (lineage, resolver list, role JSON, depth assertion, creator-person code and script, client adapter, census) ships in this task's PR and is reviewed by uac-r2 under its review gate (condition f).</constraint>
    <constraint source="design note 4.1">Create, assign and publish each apply the 4.1 matrix and return 400 ruleset.invalid (publish: ruleset.publish_invalid) with the listed field errors. Record and FirmRecord need exactly one existing core lookup; ClientDefault and Firm take none. matterId, projectId and workAssignmentId are query parameters on create and on PUT /{id}/assignment (078 doctrine, as RateCardImportEndpoints takes them). The BU ids go in RuleSetAssignmentBody (AllBusinessUnits, BusinessUnitIds); each must exist.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-141 condition 3, uac-r2 review item 6">The Secure Record business unit in BusinessUnitIds is refused with field businessUnits. It is found through UAC's named resolution, SecureChildShareSynchronizer.ResolveSecureBusinessUnitAsync; an ambiguous or unreadable resolution refuses the write (503), never treats the list as clean. Do not resolve the business unit by a name comparison of your own.</constraint>
    <constraint source="design note 4.1, root CLAUDE.md section 6">Every route that NAMES a core record in the request (create, assignment) checks, as the caller, a new OperationAccessPolicy key ebilling.ruleset.scope = Read | AppendTo, through the same FinanceAuthorizationCheck Record path RateCardImportEndpoints uses (copy it; do not reuse finance.submit_invoice or finance.read). Refusal is 403 from the filter before the service runs.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 review item 3 (REQUIREMENT), design note 4.6">Every route keyed by a rule-set id (GET /{ruleSetId}, POST /{ruleSetId}/draft, PATCH .../proposals/{policyId}, POST .../accept and .../reject, POST .../publish, PUT .../assignment, PUT .../visibility, and a retire route if one is added) reads the set's three core lookups on the server, app-only, and checks the caller's Read on that record (plus AppendTo on every write route) BEFORE anything about the set is read back or written. An unknown set id, a set the caller may not see and a set whose record the caller cannot Read all answer the SAME 404 (FinanceAuthorizationFilter.UniformRecordNotFound). A ClientDefault or Firm set needs no record check. Each route's basis is declared in RouteAuthorizationGuardTests (the GovernedFiles entry for Api/Finance/RuleSetEndpoints.cs). The record id is never taken from the request on these routes.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 round 2 item 081.7 (SHOULD)">ListAsync leaves out every record-scoped set whose core record the caller cannot Read, with one batched call per page through CallerRecordAccessProbe.GetCallerRightsForRecordsAsync; no new per-row loop.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-146 (design note 4.6 point 6; added with D-146, not a uac-r2 finding)">A Record or FirmRecord set with NO core lookup (unlinked by a core-record delete) has no record to check: on every id-keyed route the check is then the caller's Read on the SET ROW itself (plus Write on write routes), with the same uniform 404, and List applies the same rule in its batched call. It never falls back to the billing-admin privilege alone, so a Secure-owned orphan stays out of reach for a billing admin outside the Secure team.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-137 / ontology D-172 Q9">AssignAsync on a Published set that changes scope, firm, core record or BU list returns 409 ruleset.scope_locked ("Retire this rule set and create a new one for the new scope."), Firm sets included, and writes nothing.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 round 2 item 081.4 (MUST), design note 4.3">A Draft re-scope is built like InvoiceDocumentRefile, in this order: (0) step 0 confirms the #2150 fix is an ancestor of HEAD; (1) wasIsolated through RefileIsolationRead.BeforeWriteAsync before any write; a FAILED isolation read writes nothing and returns 503; (2) IRecordOwnershipResolver.ReparentAsync on the SET ONLY, with RecordReparent.SecureExitCaller set for the HTTP caller (the F3 check), and its applyChange callback writes the set's core lookups conditionally on the set's row version (a concurrent change refuses and writes nothing); (3) refusal mapping: ONLY a refusal whose code SecureDesignationRemoval.IsNotPermitted accepts maps to 403 sdap.unsecure.not_permitted; every other refusal (an unresolved team, the typed #2150 refusal, a concurrent owner change) goes through ProblemDetailsHelper.RecordOwnerRefused with its own status and code; do not hard-code the #2150 exception name or code, use the typed refusal uac-r2 confirms when #2150 merges; (4) SecureChildReconciler.AfterRefileAsync("sprk_ruleset", id, wasIsolated, CancellationToken.None) runs ONLY when the reparent result is not ParentsUnchanged; it moves the policies and versions; do NOT reparent policies or versions one by one. RuleSetService is added to RefileWriterAfterRefileGuardTests' non-vacuity list and to the census UnscannedWriters as a REPARENT.</constraint>
    <constraint source="design note 4.2">The S3 flag and the N:N associate / disassociate are written as the app identity in the same call; associates are idempotent; a partial failure is 500 ruleset.write_failed. Publish re-reads the list and refuses No with an empty list. RuleSetSummary gains AllBusinessUnits, BusinessUnitIds and coreRecord {type, id, name} (additive members).</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-171 Q3, uac-r2 review item 4, design note 4.4">Owner parents come from EACH ROW'S OWN lookups: ResolveOwnerAsync(RecordOwnershipContext.ForParents(RecordOwnershipContext.ParentsOf(row), caller) with { RequestedBy = caller }) for the set (its core lookup, or none for ClientDefault / Firm), the policy (its sprk_ruleset) and the version (its sprk_policy), at CreateAsync, AddDraftsAsync and EditAsync's new-version create. Never hand the set's core record to a policy or version create. A refusal writes nothing (409 OwnerRefused).</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-145, uac-r2 round 2 item 081.5 (MUST) and its D7 / T7 update">The rule set's source document (CreateAsync, through the CreateDocumentAsync seam): (a) a ClientDefault or Firm set's document stays unlinked in the Procedure Library, owned by the caller's business-unit team with no parent (as today, as timekeepers); (b) a Record or FirmRecord set's document is FILED under the same core record through the document's own typed lookup (sprk_matter / sprk_project / sprk_workassignment); its owner is resolved from its OWN row, ResolveOwnerAsync(ForParents(ParentsOf(docRow))) with RequestedBy = caller, never the set's owner handed over; its file is stored in that record's container (RecordContainerResolver), not the Procedure Library; and it is created with its own sprk_accesspermission = Restricted (option value read live; the roots use 100000002, ParentLineage.Restricted), so UAC T7 hides it from every external document surface (D-63 / D-66). (c) Nothing reaches an environment before T7 has shipped there. No other path hands one row's owner to another. The old behaviour (an unlinked document carrying the set's owner) is removed, not kept as a fallback.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 round 2 item 081.9 (SHOULD)">The census UnscannedWriters gains a row for RuleSetService as a CreateDocumentAsync seam caller (the source document), with its reason.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 review item 1 (binding), round 2 items 081.1-081.3, design note 5.1">ONE change (one PR) carries every part, because SecureFamilyConsistencyTests, SecureChildNewCommandAgreementTests and the census creator-person tests fail on any subset: SecureChildLineage T("sprk_ruleset", "sprk_rulesets", ("sprk_matter", Matter), ("sprk_project", Project), ("sprk_workassignment", WorkAssignment)) and ("sprk_ruleset", "sprk_ruleset") on sprk_policy; "sprk_ruleset" in OwnershipParentEntities; sprk_ruleset in OWNERSHIP_CHILD_TABLES (bffChildWriteAdapter.ts); the config/secure-record-owner-role.json row (logicalName sprk_ruleset, kind child, prvReadsprk_ruleset, Read, Basic, a reason and the verbatim probe evidence); sprk_ruleset, sprk_policy and sprk_policyversion in RecordCreatorPerson.StampedChildTables AND in scripts/Set-ChildRecordCreatorPersonSchema.ps1 (in $SolutionHomes, so the script only SECURES and VERIFIES the column; the owning schema created it: 080 for sprk_ruleset, ontology-r1 for sprk_policy and sprk_policyversion), with tests/scripts/Set-ChildRecordCreatorPersonSchema.Tests.ps1 updated in the same change; census ChildTables + Routed entries + a PersonBearingWriters entry ("RuleSetService.cs", "OwnerContext", the billing admin who asked); the NFR-05 entries for the three tables (below).</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 round 2 item 081.1 (MUST)">Do NOT append the three tables to SecureBuRoleDepthAssertion.GuardedPrivileges: RequireGuardedPrivilegesResolved throws the whole census away when any guarded name is missing (ordinal match), and SecureBuRoleDepthAssertionTests pins exactly the three roots, so one mis-cased prvReadsprk_ruleset would blind NFR-05 everywhere. Add a SEPARATE, non-throwing list graded by clause 1 in which an unresolved name becomes a FINDING naming it. uac-r2 agrees the exact shape in review. Schema-name casing read live.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 round 2 item 081.2 (MUST)">A Rule Set subgrid on the matter, project or work assignment main form: either none exists, or it is listed in ChildSubgridsOnMainForms and served by the secure-child ribbon. 079 edits those forms and must keep this true.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 review item 1 (live part), round 2 item 081.8">The role row's evidence is a LIVE refusal probe on dev: create a sprk_ruleset row with ownerid bound to the Secure Record Owners team BEFORE the privilege is added; record the verbatim 0x80040299 refusal (the #2084 shape in the sprk_policy entry); if the create succeeds, delete the probe row and STOP (escalation). Then Set-SecureRecordOwnerRolePrivileges.ps1 -WhatIf, -Apply, -Verify on dev, and Set-ChildRecordCreatorPersonSchema.ps1 dry run, -Apply, -Verify on dev, each with the owner's OK. Both -Verify runs gate EVERY target environment before a BFF carrying this code deploys there (079 for dev and the release path for any other): once the set is in the lineage, Step 8 and the reconciler re-own it, and a missing role row makes Make Secure return children_incomplete.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 review item 1 (NFR-05)">The depth check covers sprk_ruleset, sprk_policy and sprk_policyversion for EVERY non-admin role, including the role through which billing admins hold prvWritesprk_ruleset (read it live; it is not in SpaarkeMaster source). Record the live NFR-05 result (SecureRecordIsolationCensusJob or the manual gate) on dev in the notes. Basic and Core User hold Deep Read on sprk_ruleset and sprk_Policy, as on sprk_Matter; the live business-unit tree decides.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 round 2 item 081.6 (SHOULD)">Step 8 reads sprk_ruleset's OwnershipType live and records that it is UserOwned (OwnershipParentEntities requires it); anything else STOPs.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ontology-r1 Q14 condition 1 (note section 12)">In the same change: census sprk_policy / sprk_policyversion Waived → Routed, new entry ("RuleSetService.cs", "sprk_ruleset", 1, Routed), RuleSetServicePolicyWaiver deleted, sprk_ruleset in ChildTables. RuleSetServiceNoMatterScopeGuardTests replaced by the narrower guard "no sprk_matter written on a PolicyEntity or VersionEntity row". Twin guard: OntologyAdminAuthoringService never writes sprk_ruleset. Both guards have a must-fire and a must-not-fire control and a seeding proof. RuleSetServiceOwnerTests.NoWrittenPolicyOrVersionRowCarriesAMatter_OnCreateOrUpdate stays unchanged. ontology-r1 reviews both guards and the creator-person rows on its two tables.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ontology-r1 Q14 condition 2">SecureFamilyConsistencyTests and a SecureChildReconciler test cover version → policy → ruleset → core end to end, on secure and on unsecure. CitationLookups unchanged.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-146, uac-r2 core-record-delete-design.md §12 (3fbf650fb)">No delete-Restrict and no delete code in this task. Core-record delete ALWAYS keeps the record's own set (with its policies and versions), clears its core lookup (RemoveLink) and never deletes it; the set then applies to nothing (S2). No citation check is involved, so no citing-lookup list is sent to uac-r2. The delete job never re-owns these rows: a set under a Secure matter stays Secure-owned after the unlink, reachable only by the Secure team or an admin. Retiring or re-scoping such an orphaned set is a known limit (design note 4.5, K), not work in this task.</constraint>
    <constraint source=".claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md">BFF hygiene: placement (existing RuleSetService and endpoints), publish-size delta against a fresh master build, no new package, no new HIGH CVE.</constraint>
    <constraint source="uac-r2 2026-10-20 (#2150, was #2143)">Every owner write that this task routes through IRecordOwnershipResolver (create and reparent) handles the resolver's fail-closed refusal after its bounded RowVersion retries. Nothing is written, the caller gets the existing ownership-refusal error through ProblemDetailsHelper.RecordOwnerRefused (never a 500, never a partial write, never 403 sdap.unsecure.not_permitted), and a test pins the refusal path. Re-read RecordOwnershipResolver on origin/master before starting. If #2150 has not merged, STOP and ask the main session.</constraint>
  </constraints>

